  "response": "Here's a Python function that formats a number as currency with the dollar sign at the beginning and commas separating every three digits. For negative numbers, it includes a minus sign before the dollar sign:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Format a number as currency with $ prefix and thousand separators.\n    \n    Args:\n        n (int or float): The number to format.\n        \n    Returns:\n        str: Formatted currency string.\n    \"\"\"\n    sign = '-' if n < 0 else ''\n    abs_n = abs(n)\n    formatted = f'{abs_n:,}'\n    return f'${sign}{formatted}'\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))        # $4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(-12345.67))      # -$12,345.67\nprint(format_currency(0))              # $0\nprint(format_currency(100))            # $100\nprint(format_currency(-9999999))       # -$9,999,999\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- The function preserves the input type (int or float). For floats with decimal places like `-12345.67`, formatting works as expected.\n- This approach ensures correct placement of the minus sign relative to the dollar sign for all inputs, including negative floats and integers.\n- It does not depend on locale-specific settings, making it portable across environments.",
